<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Dutch Cape Colony</h1><p class="st-meta">1652 CE – 1806 CE · capital Cape Town</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Dutch East India Company's colony at the Cape of Good Hope, from which European settlement and the Afrikaans language spread across South Africa.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1652 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-van-riebeeck">Jan van Riebeeck</a> landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/table-bay">Table Bay</a> to set up a refreshment station for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">VOC</a> ships sailing to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">East Indies</a>. From 1657 the Company released servants as free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> to farm, and the settlement expanded at the expense of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khoekhoe">Khoikhoi</a> herders, who were defeated in wars in 1659 and 1673–77.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The colony was run by Company officials, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-van-der-stel">Simon van der Stel</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/willem-adriaan-van-der-stel">Willem Adriaan</a>, and its economy depended on wine, wheat and enslaved labour brought from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">East Indies</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>. French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> arrived in 1688. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trekboers">Trekboer</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> farmers pushed the frontier east to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-fish-river">Fish River</a>, where they met the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xhosa-people">Xhosa</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a> developed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-dutch">Cape Dutch</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> seized the Cape in 1795 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muizenberg">Battle of Muizenberg</a>, returned it to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/batavian-republic">Batavian Republic</a> in 1803 and took it again after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-blaauwberg">Battle of Blaauwberg</a> in 1806. It became the British <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1652 CE – 1806 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">colony</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:92.58%;width:3.06%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1652 CE – 1806 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cape Town</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jan van Riebeeck, Simon van der Stel, Willem Adriaan van der Stel</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Dutch Reformed Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dutch</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>South Africa</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cape of Good Hope (VOC), Kaapkolonie</dd></dl></aside></div>
